#!/bin/bash
# Interactive shell for the showroom OpenShift Cluster Terminal. oc is baked into
# the image; the merged kubeconfig for every deployed cluster is injected onto the
# shared disk at /showroom/kube/config (root-owned 0644 — readable, not writable).
#
# We prefer to drop to the unprivileged uid-1000 lab user via setpriv. That works
# on troshkad (podman: root + CAP_SETUID). Under KubeVirt's restricted SCC the
# container runs as root but WITHOUT CAP_SETUID, so setpriv fails ("setresuid
# failed: Operation not permitted", exit 127). Probe setpriv up front and, if it
# can't drop, fall back to a shell as the current uid.
export PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

_can_drop=""
if command -v setpriv >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    && setpriv --reuid=1000 --regid=1000 --init-groups true >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _can_drop=1
fi

if [ -n "$_can_drop" ]; then
    # Privilege drop available: prep uid-1000's home + kubeconfig, then drop.
    export HOME=/home/labuser
    export KUBECONFIG="$HOME/.kube/config"
    if [ -f /showroom/kube/config ]; then
        install -d -o 1000 -g 1000 -m 0700 "$HOME/.kube"
        install -o 1000 -g 1000 -m 0600 /showroom/kube/config "$KUBECONFIG"
    fi
    cd "$HOME" 2>/dev/null || cd /tmp
    exec setpriv --reuid=1000 --regid=1000 --init-groups /bin/bash
fi

# Fallback (KubeVirt restricted SCC): can't drop privileges — run as the current
# uid. Use a writable HOME and a kubeconfig copy the current uid can read.
if [ -z "$HOME" ] || [ ! -w "$HOME" ]; then
    export HOME=/tmp
fi
export KUBECONFIG="$HOME/.kube/config"
if [ -f /showroom/kube/config ]; then
    mkdir -p "$HOME/.kube"
    cp /showroom/kube/config "$KUBECONFIG" 2>/dev/null && chmod 0600 "$KUBECONFIG" 2>/dev/null
fi
cd "$HOME" 2>/dev/null || cd /tmp
exec /bin/bash
